    <h1>Signs Your HVAC System Is Dying vs. Worth One More Repair</h1>
    <p class="intro-lead">
      A system that stops blowing cold air in July or cold heat in January does not automatically need a $10,000 replacement. Many breakdowns trace back to $200–$500 electrical wear items, while a smaller group of failures signal genuine end-of-life or immediate safety risk.
    </p>

    <h2>Group 1: Immediate Safety Issues (Never Delay)</h2>
    <p>
      Safety overrides any repair-vs-replace dollar ratio. Shut the system down and call a licensed HVAC professional or gas utility immediately if you face any of the following:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cracked gas furnace heat exchanger or CO alarm activation:</strong> A cracked or corroded heat exchanger can leak carbon monoxide (CO) into supply air. On older furnaces outside heat-exchanger warranty, heat-exchanger replacement labor often approaches a new furnace cost, making replacement the safer choice.</li>
      <li><strong>Repeated tripped breakers, scorched wiring, or burning electrical odors:</strong> Shorted compressor windings or melting terminal lugs create electrical fire hazards and typically indicate severe compressor stress.</li>
      <li><strong>Active gas leaks or blocked flue venting:</strong> Improper combustion exhaust or damaged flue pipes require immediate red-tag shutdown and licensed repair before running the furnace again.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Group 2: Terminal or High-Risk Failures (Strong Lean Replace on Older Units)</h2>
    <p>
      When a component fails that accounts for a large share of total equipment cost—or relies on phased-out refrigerant—replacing older systems usually makes more financial sense:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Failed compressor out of warranty:</strong> The compressor is the heart of your outdoor condenser or heat pump. Replacing a burned-out compressor on a unit over 10–12 years old routinely costs several thousand dollars while leaving an aged outdoor coil, fan motor, and indoor coil untouched.</li>
      <li><strong>Major R-22 refrigerant leak:</strong> Because EPA banned virgin R-22 production and import on January 1, 2020, repairing an R-22 coil leak or recharging an R-22 system older than 12 years triggers our calculator’s hard replacement flag.</li>
      <li><strong>Simultaneous indoor coil and blower/board failures:</strong> When a system over 12–15 years old suffers back-to-back major repairs within 12 months, future repair expectations climb rapidly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Group 3: Routine Wear Parts (Usually Worth Repairing)</h2>
    <p>
      If your system runs on R-410A (or R-32), is under 12–14 years old, and has no history of chronic leaks, these common breakdowns rarely justify full replacement:
    </p>

    <div tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Common HVAC wear parts vs terminal failures" class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="estimate-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Symptom / Part</th>
            <th>Why It Happens</th>
            <th>Typical Repair vs. Replace Lean</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Dual run capacitor</th>
            <td>Heat and voltage spikes wear out the outdoor capacitor that starts the fan and compressor.</td>
            <td>Almost always <strong>repair</strong> — minor electrical wear item even at age 10+.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Contactor or relay switch</th>
            <td>Pitted electrical contacts prevent 240V power from reaching the outdoor unit.</td>
            <td>Almost always <strong>repair</strong> — inexpensive standalone switch.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Clogged condensate drain / float switch</th>
            <td>Algae buildup trips the overflow float switch and shuts off cooling to protect ceilings.</td>
            <td><strong>Repair / clean</strong> — zero mechanical wear on the AC itself.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Flame sensor or hot surface igniter</th>
            <td>Furnace lights briefly then shuts down due to soot or brittle silicon nitride igniter.</td>
            <td><strong>Repair</strong> — standard maintenance wear part on gas furnaces.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Inducer motor or PSC blower motor</th>
            <td>Bearings wear out after 8–12 years of daily airflow cycles.</td>
            <td>Check with calculator — usually <strong>repair</strong> on healthy R-410A units unless paired with a cracked heat exchanger.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Worked Example: Two Calls on the Same Hot Week</h2>
    <div class="worked-example">
      <p>
        Compare two homeowners whose air conditioning stopped cooling on the same afternoon:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Homeowner A (9-year-old R-410A split AC):</strong> Outdoor fan isn’t spinning; tech diagnoses a swollen dual run capacitor and pitted contactor, quoting <strong>$360</strong> for repair and pitching a <strong>$9,500</strong> replacement “since it’s almost 10 years old.” Check A = <code>9 × $360 = $3,240</code> (under $5,000), Check B = <code>3.8%</code> (under 50%), Check C = clear, and 5-year holding cost favors repairing by over $8,000. <strong>Clear Repair.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Homeowner B (15-year-old R-22 split AC):</strong> Indoor coil is corroded and leaking, compressor amperage spikes above locked-rotor specs, and the tech quotes <strong>$3,200</strong> to swap the coil and recharge reclaimed R-22 versus <strong>$10,400</strong> for a full matched replacement. Check A = <code>15 × $3,200 = $48,000</code>, Check C = fired (age &gt; 12 + R-22), and the outdoor compressor is already showing electrical stress. <strong>Clear Replace.</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Don’t Guess Under Summer or Winter Pressure</h2>
    <p>
      Emergency repair visits happen in the worst weather, which makes it tempting to say yes to the first option offered. Take five minutes to get a written diagnosis naming the exact failed part, ask whether a standard 1-year or 2-year parts-and-labor warranty backs that repair, and check the outdoor nameplate for both the manufacture year and refrigerant stamp before making a commitment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once you have those facts in hand, enter both numbers into our <a href="/">HVAC Repair or Replace Calculator</a>, review <a href="/guides/5000-rule/">our guide to the $5,000 and 50% rules</a>, and inspect <a href="/guides/how-to-read-hvac-quote/">what a complete quote should list</a> so you separate genuine end-of-life mechanical failures from sales pressure. If the repair requires a full replacement quote, verify contractor credentials and ACCA Manual J sizing using <a href="/guides/how-to-vet-hvac-contractor/">our contractor vetting guide</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
